<div class="content">
  <h1>Direct access to weather data</h1>
  <p>
    The <a href="/api/">JSON API</a> is convenient for small point queries. Use direct access to
    the catalog's Icechunk Zarr stores for larger time ranges, many locations, repeated analysis,
    or dimensions and products the API does not support. Your code selects and processes the data
    with xarray instead of asking the API to do it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A budget refusal means you can reduce variables, times, runs or batch size, or use direct
    access. For a <code>429</code>, reduce concurrency and honor <code>Retry-After</code> before
    retrying. Direct access avoids the point API's compute and response limits; it does not bypass
    data-source rate limits, geographic restrictions or terms. Start with a small selection.
  </p>

  <h2>Find a dataset</h2>
  <p>
    Open the <a href="/catalog/">Dynamical catalog</a>, choose a dataset, and copy its ID.
    Its page lists variables, dimensions, units, coverage, examples, license and validation notes.
    Use the ID with <code>dynamical_catalog.open</code>; the package resolves the current storage
    location from <a href="https://stac.dynamical.org/catalog.json">STAC</a>. Do not copy a storage
    bucket or versioned repository URL into long-lived code. Catalog availability and API query
    support are different: check the API's <code>capabilities</code> when using the API.
  </p>

  <h2>Open a dataset and extract a point</h2>
  <p>
    Use Python 3.12 or newer and the current <code>dynamical-catalog</code> package with Icechunk 2.
    Save the example below as <code>direct_access.py</code> and run it with uv:
  </p>
  {% frameHighlight "bash" %}uv run --with "dynamical-catalog>=1.1.0" --with "icechunk>=2,<3" --with xarray direct_access.py{% endframeHighlight %}
  <p>
    This reads one variable at the nearest grid point to Chicago, for the first three lead times
    of the latest <a href="/catalog/noaa-gfs-forecast/">GFS forecast</a>. Opening the dataset reads
    metadata; selecting before <code>load()</code> keeps the weather-data read bounded.
  </p>
  {% frameHighlight "py" %}import dynamical_catalog

ds = dynamical_catalog.open("noaa-gfs-forecast", chunks=None)
point = (
    ds["temperature_2m"]
    .isel(init_time=-1, lead_time=slice(0, 3))
    .sel(latitude=41.88, longitude=-87.63, method="nearest")
)
point.load()
print(point)
print(point.attrs.get("units")){% endframeHighlight %}
  <p>
    <code>init_time</code> chooses the forecast run; <code>lead_time</code> is time after that run.
    For reproducible work, replace <code>isel(init_time=-1)</code> with an exact initialization time
    from <code>ds.init_time</code>. Analysis datasets generally use <code>time</code> instead.
    Check the selected coordinates: nearest means nearest grid cell, not interpolation. This
    example uses GFS's latitude/longitude coordinates; other datasets can use projected or
    two-dimensional coordinates and need their own spatial selection.
  </p>

  <h2>WeatherNext 3: choose a statistic</h2>
  <p>
    WeatherNext 3 forecasts are not supported by the point API. Once a WN3 dataset is listed in
    the production catalog, open its ID directly. WN3 has a <code>statistic</code> axis rather than
    interchangeable ensemble members. Inspect its labels and choose one, such as <code>mean</code>,
    before loading weather values:
  </p>
  {% frameHighlight "py" %}wn3 = dynamical_catalog.open(
    "google-weathernext3-forecast-48-hour-0-1-degree-virtual", chunks=None
)
print(wn3.statistic.values)
mean = wn3["temperature_2m"].sel(statistic="mean")
print(mean.dims)  # Select a run, lead times and spatial indices before load().{% endframeHighlight %}
  <p>
    This selects a statistic but does not load the full forecast. Follow the dataset's examples
    for its <code>y</code>/<code>x</code> spatial grid; do not apply the GFS latitude/longitude
    selector unchanged. Pressure-level variables also need a level selection. The API continues
    to support WeatherNext 2 subject to its existing access checks and query limits.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep reads manageable</h2>
  <p>
    Choose variables and slice runs, times, statistics and spatial dimensions before calling
    <code>load()</code>, <code>compute()</code> or exporting. Storage reads whole chunks: a single
    requested point can still require a large chunk. Check chunk shapes and validation notes on
    the dataset page, reuse opened datasets, and process large jobs in bounded pieces rather than
    loading an entire archive into memory. <code>chunks=None</code> avoids requiring Dask; it does
    not make an unbounded selection small. Preserve missing values and check coverage rather
    than treating them as a successful complete forecast.
  </p>

  <h2>Terms and attribution</h2>
  <p>
    Read the dataset's License and Attribution sections and its STAC <code>license</code> links.
    Terms vary by dataset and can differ between historical and real-time data. WeatherNext
    includes <a href="https://storage.googleapis.com/weathernext-public/terms-of-use.pdf">Google
    DeepMind's data terms</a>; use the current links on the dataset page for the applicable terms.
    Direct access does not change those terms or geographic restrictions. Do not put service
    tokens in shared code or persisted Icechunk repository configuration.
  </p>
</div>
